@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Side-by-side comparison of the same logical capability
'                 packaged as one monolithic tree versus several independent
'                 microservice packages
'   usecase:      Educational example showing how packaging choices reshape a
'                 package diagram
'   complexity:   medium
'   domain:       E-commerce
' ============================================================================
title Example Package Diagram - Variant: Monolith vs Microservices Packaging

' ============================================================================
' PACKAGING VARIANT: MONOLITH vs MICROSERVICES
' The SAME logical capabilities (catalog, cart, orders, payments) can be
' packaged two radically different ways. This diagram uses two labeled frames
' to place the two styles side by side for comparison.
'
' Key visual differences:
'   MONOLITH     - one root package tree; sub-packages share a deployment.
'                  Internal edges are <<access>> within a single boundary.
'   MICROSERVICES- several independent root packages, each its own deployment.
'                  Edges between them cross process boundaries and represent
'                  network calls / async events (still modeled as dependencies).
' ============================================================================

' ============================ LEFT SIDE: MONOLITH ===========================
together {
frame "Monolith: single deployable unit" as MonolithFrame {

  package "ShopMonolith" {

    package "Catalog" {
      class ProductCatalog
    }

    package "Cart" {
      class ShoppingCart
    }

    package "Orders" {
      class OrderService
    }

    package "Payments" {
      class Checkout
    }

    ' In a monolith these are in-process calls - direct, synchronous.
    Catalog .down.> Cart : in-process
    Cart .down.> Orders : in-process
    Orders .down.> Payments : in-process
  }
}
}

' =========================== RIGHT SIDE: MICROSERVICES ======================
together {
frame "Microservices: many deployable units" as ServicesFrame {

  package "CatalogService" <<microservice>> {
    class ProductCatalog
    interface CatalogApi
  }

  package "CartService" <<microservice>> {
    class ShoppingCart
    interface CartApi
  }

  package "OrderService" <<microservice>> {
    class OrderProcessor
    interface OrderApi
  }

  package "PaymentService" <<microservice>> {
    class PaymentGateway
    interface PaymentApi
  }

  ' Cross-service edges now span PROCESS boundaries.
  ' Model them as dependencies, but annotate the transport.
  CatalogApi ..> CartApi : HTTPS / REST
  CartApi ..> OrderApi : async event
  OrderApi ..> PaymentApi : gRPC
}
}

' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
' COMPARISON NOTE - explains the trade-off directly on the diagram.
' ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
note bottom of MonolithFrame
  <b>Monolith trade-offs</b>
  + Single deployment, simple local calls
  + Easy code reuse and refactoring
  - Must redeploy everything on any change
  - Harder to scale pieces independently
end note

note bottom of ServicesFrame
  <b>Microservice trade-offs</b>
  + Independent deploy & scale per service
  + Clear team/service ownership boundaries
  - Network latency + partial-failure handling
  - Operational complexity (discovery, tracing)
end note

@enduml
